  {
    "episode_id": 81549,
    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/8/1/81549.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 8,
    "turn_text": "Yeah, that's true. And look, how we get there is we have a legislature that is so dominated by one ideology, by one party that there's no need to even consider ballots anymore. And for a long time, we have been an anecdote driven legislature. And that is, it's not the numbers. It's not even really, it's this horrible story happening. Sometimes not even here in Colorado. I'll give you an example. There is the Gash darn it, his name escapes me out of New York, who was illegally selling cigarettes on the street, cops came put a chokehold on him, and he died. horrible, incredibly tragic, unnecessary. And the fact that it happened should be addressed in New York here in Colorado. We had legislators say, let's outlaw choke holds by cops. I don't know a single agency up to that point that had used choke holds, trained until, but it didn't matter or an anecdote driven legislature. And so in the post George Floyd era, we saw a real turn against law enforcement, a real skepticism of law enforcement and the longer term impacts of that are just the tools that are missing out of the toolbox. But it has attacked morale such that we now see issues with retention of law enforcement officers, recruiting of law enforcement officers. All of those things combined add to an environment where we're just not as competitive as our neighbors when it comes to",
