        "text": "Outside of a conviction for first-degree murder resulting in life without parole, no one can determine the minimum prison time for any conviction.",
        "confidence": 0.96
      },
      {
        "text": "Publicly reported sentences, such as '12 years in DOC,' are inaccurate representations of actual time served.",
        "confidence": 0.95
      },
      {
        "text": "The Department of Corrections cannot specify the minimum time a prisoner will serve.",
        "confidence": 0.94
      },
      {
        "text": "Judges cannot determine the minimum prison time a convict will serve.",
        "confidence": 0.93
      },
      {
        "text": "An 85% mandatory minimum service rule would provide legal certainty for communities, victims, and offenders.",
        "confidence": 0.93
      },
      {
        "text": "For violent crimes involving human victims, no one exiting the courtroom knows the minimum prison time the offender will serve.",
        "confidence": 0.92
      },
      {
        "text": "Current statutes require serving 75% of a prison sentence for violent crimes.",
        "confidence": 0.91
      },
      {
        "text": "A 12-year sentence does not equate to nine years actually served due to various mitigating factors.",
        "confidence": 0.9
      },
      {
